  description="Why postiee exists: LinkedIn will not let any API read your own posts back, so the local record is the product."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Why</p>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    This tool exists because of one closed permission.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>

  <p>
    You post something. You cannot find it again. LinkedIn's own search has no
    "your posts" filter, and once a post is deleted it is unfindable there too.
  </p>

  <p>
    So you go looking for a tool that keeps your post history. You find a few
    things instead:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>LinkedIn's data export</strong> — a static archive, requested by
      hand, desktop-only, with a 72-hour download window. Not searchable, not
      ongoing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scheduling tools</strong> — Buffer, Hootsuite, Taplio, and the
      rest. Buffer and Hootsuite use the official API; several others drive a
      browser session, which risks the account. Hootsuite starts at
      $99/user/month to schedule posts on a platform that gives you a scheduler
      for free.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unofficial CLIs</strong> — some are good, but they scrape with
      your session cookies. Some of their users report bans six months into
      apparently-clean automation.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    One project came close: an MIT-licensed MCP server using the official API
    with a local SQLite log of created posts. But it stores the post text without
    any way to query it, and its <code>list_my_posts</code> reads only that local
    database — never LinkedIn — so anything you posted before installing it is
    invisible.
  </p>

  <h2>The specific thing that makes it impossible</h2>

  <p>
    LinkedIn has a permission called <code>r_member_social</code> for reading
    your own posts. It is not slow, not paid, and not partner-gated.
    <strong>It is closed</strong> — the documentation says it is not accepting
    access requests at this time.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the whole problem in one line. You can write to LinkedIn through an
    approved app, but no approved app can read back what it wrote. And LinkedIn's
    own native scheduler, which does let you browse your history, is a
    web product with no API.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    The practical consequence: if you lose a post's ID at creation time, that
    post is gone. Not hidden, not archived — gone. Everything in postiee follows
    from taking that seriously.
  </p>

  <h2>What that implies for the design</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The post ID is the first-class concern.</strong> It comes from a
      response header because the body is empty. If it is missing, the tool
      fails loudly instead of reporting a success it cannot back up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The local record is the product, not a feature.</strong> SQLite
      with full-text search, because a log you cannot query is not a history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing ships that cannot work.</strong> No comment command that
      403s, no analytics stub. The absence list is longer than the feature list,
      and each omission says why.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No scraping, ever.</strong> Official API only. Your credentials go
      to <code>api.linkedin.com</code> and nowhere else.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>

  <p>
    It is not a growth tool, a scheduler, or an analytics dashboard. It is a
    small, correct program that posts and remembers — sized to what one person
    can actually use, running on Go with no runtime dependencies.
  </p>

  <h2>How it was built</h2>

  <p>
    The scaffold came from
    <a href="https://github.com/mvanhorn/cli-printing-press">CLI Printing
    Press</a>, which generates Go CLIs with a local store and offline search. The
    LinkedIn client, the history layer, credential handling, and every correction
    to the generated code were hand-written.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two of those corrections are the reason this works at all: capturing the post
    ID from the response header, and resolving the member URN at runtime instead
    of accepting it as a parameter. Both were absent from the generated output.
    A third — that image upload has no finalize step — was caught while writing
    the spec.
  </p>

